# Leetspeak, extended.
#
# Seen regularly, but well behind the common set. Give it alongside that one rather than instead of
# it. b and q are here rather than in the common set: b to 8 and b to 6 are read as a b far less
# reliably than a 3 is read as an e, and q to 9 is the least common of the lot.
#
# The leetspeak tables are meant to be given together, as many as you want the run to cost:
#
#   hashcat -m 0 -a 5 hashes.txt words.txt tables/leetspeak-common.table
#   hashcat -m 0 -a 5 hashes.txt words.txt tables/leetspeak-common.table tables/leetspeak-extended.table
#
# Tables given together are read into one set, so a letter named by more than one of them ends up
# with the union of what they say. Combine these with tables/toggle.table for case as well.
#
# One rule to a line: the source token, a tab, then what to put in its place. Leaving a letter as
# it was is always one of the choices, so there are no rules here mapping a letter to itself.

b	8
c	(
c	{
c	[
c	<
g	6
i	|
l	|
l	7
t	+
x	%
z	2
b	6
q	9

B	8
C	(
C	{
C	[
C	<
G	6
I	|
L	|
L	7
T	+
X	%
Z	2
B	6
Q	9

0	o
